  "title": "The Most Expensive Mistake I See Buyers Make (And How to Avoid It)",
  "date": "2025-08-26T14:31:24.786Z",
  "content": {
    "text": "96\n\n The Most Expensive Mistake I See Buyers Make (And How to Avoid It)\nHi Sara,\nGuys, please stop handing out your equity ownership like it\u2019s candy.\u00a0\nI spend a good chunk of my workweek trying to un-mess partnership disasters. \nSo here\u2019s the one thing I wish every business owner, buyer, and entrepreneur would stop doing this year:\n\ud83d\udc49 Bringing on a \u201cpartner\u201d too early or too thoughtlessly.\nSounds harmless, right? You find someone smart, they\u2019re excited, you click. Why not share the load? You split the risk, you gain a sounding board, you finally stop annoying your spouse with endless business talk. What could go wrong?\nWell\u2026everything.\nThe Problem with Equity Promises\nMost people give away ownership based on potential: \u201cIf we build this together, we\u2019ll win together.\u201d But what actually happens is this: one person ends up doing the heavy lifting while the other disappears to become a rock-climbing, kombucha-making, off-the-grid nomad who still collects 50% of the profits forever.\nIt\u2019s not just frustrating\u2014it\u2019s legally sticky. You can\u2019t just \u201cunpartner\u201d someone because they stopped showing up. You\u2019ll need a court order or a written agreement (that they\u2019re willing to sign), and those aren\u2019t easy to get. It\u2019s often referred to as a business divorce\u2014and not just for the emotional mess it leaves behind.\nWhat Disgruntled Founders Try Instead:\n\nSpin up a competing business and channel all your energy there (not without risk).\n\nCrank up your salary to minimize profit distributions (not without risk and also taxable).\n\nSell the business anyway and watch half the money go to your absentee co-founder (infuriating).\n\nSound familiar?\nThere\u2019s a Better Way\nIf you must bring in a co-owner, treat it like a prenup. Set clear expectations, outline exit terms, and protect the business if things go sideways. (Yes, I draft those kinds of documents all the time.)\nBut if you\u2019re early in the game\u2014building or buying a business\u2014try literally anything else before giving away equity. Gift cards. Cash. Consulting fees. Free snacks. Future bonuses. Whatever. Just don\u2019t give away the whole farm because you\u2019re short on cash.\nYou\u2019ll thank me later. Your lawyer will thank you now.\nTo deals that stay drama-free,\nSara Sharp\nPartner, SK&S Law Group\nFounder, Deal Academy\nP.S. If this resonated (even if it stung a little), check out Deal Academy. Learn how to structure deals that don\u2019t end in regret.\n\n For more helpful lessons from Deal Academy, check out our Dealmakers Youtube Channel where you'll find Sara's Weekly Wine Down, where she talks about what she's seen in the industry this week and interviews with M&A Experts and Dealmakers.\nYou can check out some recent Dealmakers YouTube videos by clicking the images below.\n\n Copyright \u00a9 2025\u00a0 Someday Consultants, All rights reserved. \n\n Our e-mail address is:\ninfo@dealacademy.org\n\n Want to change how you receive these emails?\n You can unsubscribe from this list.",
